foundation.solution_text:
$0.15+0.4+0.3+0.15=1$, so the heights of the four bars must add to $1$.
Draw a bar of height $P(x)$ above each value of $x$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8.6,0);
\foreach \y/\lbl in {1/0.1,2/0.2,3/0.3,4/0.4} {\draw (-0.2,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\lbl$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.75,0) rectangle (2.25,1.5);
\draw (2.55,0) rectangle (4.05,4);
\draw (4.35,0) rectangle (5.85,3);
\draw (6.15,0) rectangle (7.65,1.5);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.5,0) {$1$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.3,0) {$2$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5.1,0) {$3$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6.9,0) {$4$};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.4,2) {$P(X=x)$};
\node[below] at (4.2,-1.0) {Books borrowed};
\node at (4.2,5.1) {Books borrowed in a day};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
